PHOTO-011 is COMPLETE (phase 3 on branch feat/PHOTO-011-brush-gradients —
check `gh pr list` before assuming it's merged). All three phases are in:
selections, adjustment layers, and brush/gradient masks.

What phase 3 added, and what's worth knowing before touching masks again:
- Two new op kinds in PhotoSelection (utils/selection.ts), alongside
  'polygon' and 'wand':
  - 'stroke': painted path (normalized source points) + radius (a fraction
    of the source's shorter edge, MAX_BRUSH_FRACTION) + hardness. Rasterized
    as a round-capped path on the scratch canvas, then the soft edge comes
    from featherMask at (100 - hardness)% of the radius.
  - 'gradient': 'linear' (graduated) or 'radial', from full coverage at
    `from` to nothing at `to`, painted with a canvas gradient.
  Both work as the base selection or as any layer's mask, and both go
  through the same add/subtract compositing as the other ops.
- Brush strokes always ADD (Alt erases) regardless of the New/Add/Subtract
  mode — replacing on every pointer-down made painting useless. Gradients
  still respect the combine mode.
- Radii map source→render through the plan matrix (sqrt of its determinant),
  not through the output size: a crop changes the size without scaling, so
  using plan.width there makes the brush the wrong thickness. The live
  preview width uses the same maths.
- Every mask kind is stored in normalized source coordinates, so masks
  survive later geometry changes.

Known limitations, deliberate:
- The mask is rebuilt from all ops whenever it changes, so a mask painted
  from very many strokes gets slower to re-rasterize (cached per selection
  object and render size, so only on change).
- The wand samples the original photo's colours, not the adjusted preview.
- The selection outline is static dashes, not animated marching ants.

UX-029 (masking flow legibility) followed PHOTO-011 on branch
feat/photo-masking-usability: labelled tools, a numbered step hint, a red
mask tint (store flag `showMask`, drawn in SelectionLayer's outline pass), a
brush-size ring that follows the pointer, and an "Adjust this area
separately" button that calls addLayer() and scrolls to #photo-adjust-anchor.
The feature was complete before it; what was missing was telling anyone how
to use it. Worth remembering for the next photo tool: ship the flow, not
just the capability.

